<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Devil's Island</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Île du Diable</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Devil's Island is the smallest of the three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iles-du-salut">Îles du Salut</a> off the coast of French Guiana. From 1852 to 1953 it formed part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>'s penal colony and was used especially for political prisoners, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-dreyfus">Alfred Dreyfus</a>, held there from 1895 to 1899. Its harsh conditions made the name a byword for brutal imprisonment.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/devils-island.jpg" alt="Devil's Island" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Arria Belli · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tour_dreyfus_kourou.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In the 16th and 17th centuries French convicts were sentenced to serve as galley slaves, which was virtually a death sentence. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">galleys</a> of the Levant Fleet were decommissioned in 1666, prisoners were kept chained on hulks moored in harbours until the hulks rotted and sank. The French Navy complained of the cost and of the disruption to the shipyards, and after his coup in 1851 Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a> ordered the hulks closed and civil-law convicts sent overseas.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominican-republic">Dominican Republic</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a> were considered before the government chose French Guiana. France had repeatedly failed to colonise it, and about 75 per cent of the 12,000 colonists sent in 1763 died in their first year. In 1852 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> called for volunteers from the hulks, and 3,000 convicts applied.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The penal colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cayenne">Cayenne</a> included sites on the mainland at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kourou">Kourou</a>, Cayenne and St. Laurent, and three islands in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iles-du-salut">Salvation Islands</a>. Île Royale received the general population, Saint-Joseph Island was the Reclusion where inmates were punished by solitary confinement in silence and darkness, and Devil's Island held political prisoners.</p><p>Convicts were either transportés, civil-law prisoners sentenced under doublage, which required them to work in the colony for a period equal to their sentence afterwards, or déportés, convicted of political crimes. A law of 30 May 1854 required those whose sentence exceeded eight years to remain in French Guiana for life. Among the inmates were 239 republicans who had opposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a>'s coup.</p><p>More than 80,000 prisoners were sent to the system, and the vast majority never returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Disease, mosquitoes and harsh treatment killed many, and the death rate was 75 per cent at its worst. Few escaped, since the only way out of the island prisons was by water. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-dreyfus">Alfred Dreyfus</a>, unjustly convicted of treason, was sent to Devil's Island on 5 January 1895, and the Dreyfus affair publicised the horrors of the system.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>In 1938 René Belbenoît's book Dry Guillotine aroused public outrage, and the French government announced plans to close the colony. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> delayed this, but the prisons were closed one by one from 1946 until 1953, and the Devil's Island facility was the last. Henri Charrière's bestselling book Papillon of 1969 describes his escape from the island with a companion, though they were recaptured. Devil's Island is now closed to the public, but the two larger islands have restored prison buildings that serve as museums.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Devil's Island” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Still protesting his innocence, he was sent to <b>Devil's Island</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-5">5 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>From 1852 to 1953 the colony held a penal colony, the bagne, including the Îles du Salut, where Alfred Dreyfus was held on <b>Devil's Island</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-guiana">French Guiana</a></li></ul></section>
